FINDINGS = [
 ("F1","Background","Chapter 11 of the 3rd edition, Organizing Files, teaches the shutil module with shutil.copy(), shutil.copytree() and shutil.move() for copying, moving and renaming; permanent deletion with os.unlink(), os.rmdir() and shutil.rmtree(), with a dry run as protection and the third-party send2trash module as the recoverable alternative; listing a folder with os.listdir() and iterdir() and walking a tree with os.walk(), shown by a program that renames every file to upper case; the zipfile module, with ZipFile objects in write, append and read use, namelist(), getinfo() with file_size and compress_size, extractall() and extract(); Project 5, backup_to_zip.py, which writes a folder into a ZIP file whose name counts up; and it closes with a summary, 4 practice questions and 4 practice programs: selective copying, finding large files, renumbering files and converting American to European dates.",["P01"]),
 ("F2","Comparative analysis","The chapter's printed outputs reproduce under Python 3.14.4 except one sentence: shutil.copy() returned a str when the destination was a folder and a PosixPath when it was a file name, which is what the chapter prints; the walk of the chapter gave the same folders and files on each pass, but the documentation says that the order of names depends on the file system, so the chapter's listing is one possible order and the runs here sorted the names; the ZIP listing reproduced the member name file1.txt, file_size 50000, compress_size 97 and Compressed file is 515.46x smaller!, while the chapter's prose puts the compressed size at about 213 bytes, a number that neither compresslevel 9 nor the default level 6 gave (both 97; level 1 gave 293 and level 0 gave 50010), so the prose and the listing disagree with each other and only the listing runs.",["P01","P02","P03","P04","P07"]),
 ("F3","Comparative analysis","The chapter says that a file1.txt already in the destination folder of shutil.move() would be overwritten; under Python 3.14.4 shutil.move() into a folder that already held file1.txt raised shutil.Error and moved nothing, which is what the documentation states (the destination path in the folder must not already exist), whereas a move onto an existing path that is not a folder replaced the file; the chapter also describes the return value of shutil.move() as a string, and it was a str for a folder destination and a PosixPath for a new file name.",["P01","P02"]),
 ("F4","Comparative analysis","The chapter's Project 5 program differs from what its prose intends in four ways that were run: write() without a compression type stores the file, so the snapshot is not compressed although the chapter speaks of compressing; passing absolute paths to write() stores the whole path of each file without its leading slash as the member name; giving the folder as Path('.') raises IndexError, because Path('.').parts is an empty tuple; and when the archive is created inside the folder being backed up, os.walk lists the archive and the program writes it into itself; the program also tests names in the working directory and not next to the folder.",["P01","P03","P04","P05"]),
 ("F5","Comparative analysis","The chapter's advice on deletion holds: os.unlink() deletes a file for good, os.rmdir() only an empty folder (OSError otherwise) and shutil.rmtree() a whole tree, and the dry run, in which the delete is replaced by a print, exposed the typo *.rxt for *.txt by matching no file; current Python adds that unlink() on a folder raises IsADirectoryError on this Linux system, that rmtree() on a missing path raises FileNotFoundError unless ignore_errors=True, and that the onexc parameter, added in Python 3.12, replaces the deprecated onerror as the way to handle errors while deleting a tree; send2trash was not executed.",["P01","P02","P04"]),
 ("F6","Contemporary developments","Current Python gives file handling more than the chapter presents: shutil.copy2() keeps metadata such as the modification time and copytree() uses it, copytree(dirs_exist_ok=True) copies into an existing folder, Path objects are accepted by the shutil and os functions since PEP 519 and have iterdir(), glob() and rglob(), Path.walk() since Python 3.12 is os.walk() for Path objects, and since Python 3.14 Path.copy(), copy_into(), move() and move_into() copy and move without shutil.",["P02","P05","P09","P10","P11","P12"]),
 ("F7","Contemporary developments","Current Python gives archives more than the chapter presents: ZipFile mode 'x' refuses to overwrite and 'a' adds, ZipFile.mkdir() since Python 3.11 adds a folder entry, compression methods beyond deflate include ZIP_BZIP2, ZIP_LZMA and, new in Python 3.14, ZIP_ZSTANDARD, shutil.make_archive() and shutil.unpack_archive() do in one call what the chapter does with a program, extraction removes .. and leading slashes from member names so that the members of a hostile archive stay inside the target folder, and the documentation still warns never to extract untrusted archives without inspection.",["P02","P03","P07"]),
 ("F8","Conclusion","For an advanced course, chapter 11 is best taught by asking of each operation whether it can be undone, whether it stays inside the folder it was given, and whether the program can show what it will do before it does it: copy and copytree keep the source and move does not, rmtree, unlink and rmdir cannot be undone and send2trash can; the chapter's outputs hold under 3.14.4 except the overwrite claim for shutil.move and the 213-byte sentence, and Project 5 needs its compression type, its archive names, its folder argument and the place of its archive fixed, while pathlib's walk, copy and move, the exclusive and append modes, make_archive and the safety of extraction are what the chapter leaves for the course to add.",["P01","P02","P03","P04","P05"]),
]
